Cheers,

I'm new to Stan and was pointed it's direction to deal with measurement error because I have two measurement that I would like to model with a line. There is no sure way to know which is dependent/independent but the method from the user manual in section 10.1 seemed to be the right way to go. However, I do have an estimate of the errors, and before allowing the data to determine them (i.e. leaving them as parameters of the model) I wanted to try with some simulated to first put the errors in by hand. The model and simulated data assumed homoscedastic variance in both directions and everything worked nicely.

Next, I wanted to extend the example to heteroscedastic variance. First putting the assumed errors in by hand:

model_code = """
data {
    int<lower=0> N; // number of points
    vector[N] y; //
    vector[N] x_meas;     // measurement of x
    vector[N] xsig;
    vector[N] ysig;
}

parameters {

     vector[N] x; //unknown true value
     real mu_x; //prior location
     real sigma_x; //prior scale

     real alpha_perp;
     real<lower=-pi()/2, upper=pi()/2> theta;
}

transformed parameters {
    real alpha;
    real beta;
    vector[N] ymodel;
    alpha <- alpha_perp / cos(theta);
    beta <- sin(theta);

    ymodel <- alpha + beta * x;
    }

model {

    x ~ normal(mu_x, sigma_x); // prior
    x_meas ~ normal(x,xsig); // measurement model
    y ~ normal(ymodel, ysig);

} """

This ends up being very unstable and producing bi-modal distributions for alpha and beta. One mode is on what I would expect, and the other is almost perpendicular to the simulated line. I'm new at this so my model may be ill-formed. There are no errors in the Stan output, so I'm assuming my extension of this example is wrong. Ideas? Additionally, If I leave xsig and ysig as parameters then the whole thing become unconstrained.

Furthermore, I'd like to extend this to a case where I am fitting multiple data sets to the same line by assuming a hyperprior on all the independent models:

model_meta_code = """
data {
    int<lower=0> N; // number of points
    int<lower=0> Ngroups;
    vector[N] y; //
    vector[N] x_meas;     // measurement of x
    //vector[N] xsig;
    vector[N] ysig;

    int group[N];

    //real xsig;
    //real ysig;
}

parameters {

     vector[N] x; //unknown true value
     //vector[Ngroups] mu_x; //prior location
     //vector<lower=0>[Ngroups] sigma_x; //prior scale

     vector[Ngroups] alpha;
     vector[Ngroups] beta;

     // Group mu parameters

     real alpha_mu_meta;
     real beta_mu_meta;

     // Group sigma parameters

     real alpha_sig_meta;
     real beta_sig_meta;

}

model {

    //for(i in 1:N){
    //x[i] ~ normal(mu_x[group[i]], sigma_x[group[i]]); // prior
    //}

    //x_meas ~ normal(x,xsig); // measurement model

    for(i in 1:N){
    y[i] ~ normal( alpha[group[i]] + beta[group[i]] * x_meas[i], ysig[i]);
    }

    for(j in 1:Ngroups){

    alpha[j] ~ normal(alpha_mu_meta,1./(alpha_sig_meta)^2);
    beta[j]  ~ normal(beta_mu_meta,1./(beta_sig_meta)^2);
    }
    alpha_mu_meta ~ normal(0,3);
    beta_mu_meta  ~ normal(0,3);

    alpha_sig_meta ~ uniform(1.0E-3,1.0E+3);
    beta_sig_meta ~ uniform(1.0E-3,1.0E+3);

} """

This works if I don't account for measurement errors (the commented lines) and if I put the "assumed errors" in instead of leaving them as parameters. If I eitther allow for measurement error or leave the variance as free parameters everything falls apart.

Any ideas where I'm going wrong? So far I'm just working with some simulated data I generate in python.

Thanks in advance!

Why not just fit a bivariate normal distribution to observed variables x and y? This would be quite straightforward, as it only requires you to estimate mu_x, mu_y, var_x, var_y, and covar_xy (or rho_xy, see manual for examples on how to best parameterise a MVN distribution, although it matters very little for the bivariate case). The slope of the line you are looking for is simply var_y / var_x. In linear regression (where we assume x is observed without error), the slope of the line is also var_y / var_x, but multiplied by rho_xy so as to account for the assumption of x being observed without error (see Wikipedia <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multivariate_normal_distribution#Conditional_distributions>).

