On Monday, November 30, 2015 at 4:54:55 AM UTC-5, andy...@cubist-asia.com wrote:

> Question:
> 1. is it possible to access variable declared in data & parameters block from user defined functions?

No, unless you pass them as arguments

> 2. If not, what would be the way to do posterior simulation with expose_stan_functions?

Pass the posterior draws as another argument. Something like

vector stan_gen_rng(matrix beta, vector sigma, vector x_tilde) {
  vector[rows(beta)] y_tilde;
  # beta has sims rows and K columns as in extract(stanfit, "beta")[[1]]
  y_tilde <- beta * x_tilde;
  for (i in 1:rows(beta))
    y_tilde[i] <- y_tilde[i] + normal_rng(0, sigma[i]);
  return y_tilde;
}

Ben

Hi,

That's great; thanks.  It makes a huge difference to the speed the model runs at too :-) .

Your message seems to have got cut off at the screen shot in the Google Groups post, but did come through in the email copy I got.  I've pasted the missing bit below, for completeness.

Thanks again for all your help with this.

David

The Stan estimates (our sigma_delta is their sigma), using defaults with 10K
iterations per chain:

              mean se_mean    sd   2.5%    25%    50%    75%  97.5% n_eff  Rhat
sigma_delta  0.110   0.001 0.068  0.008  0.058  0.102  0.151  0.267  3361 1.001
d           -0.253   0.001 0.065 -0.376 -0.296 -0.254 -0.211 -0.121  7508 1.000

Not sure how many chains they ran, so I'm not sure how to compare MCMC
std errors (MC_error for OpenBUGS, se_mean for Stan).

Stan's 2.5% quantile for sigma_delta is about 1/3 of that provided by
OpenBUGS, though the 97.5% quantile is closer.

Lowering step size and increasing target acceptance rate to

  control=list(stepsize=0.001, adapt_delta=0.999)

produces results that are within estimation error, though a little lower
for sigma_delta:

              mean se_mean    sd   2.5%    25%    50%    75%  97.5% n_eff Rhat
sigma_delta  0.106   0.001 0.067  0.007  0.056  0.098  0.146  0.260  4337    1
d           -0.254   0.001 0.065 -0.380 -0.297 -0.255 -0.213 -0.124 12850    1

The OpenBUGS description emphasizes this model's sensitivity to prior choice.

- Bob

P.S.  Our current recommendations for priors are here:

  https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Prior-Choice-Recommendations

and in the Stan user's guide and reference manual chapter on regression.

P.P.S.  Here's the writeup on the OpenBUGS page:

http://mathstat.helsinki.fi/openbugs/Examples/Blockers.html

